WHAT THIS IS, AND WHERE IT CAME FROM
------------------------------------

rusted-empire is a modified version of VMS-Empire, rewritten in Rust with
a ratatui terminal interface. It is a derivative work of the C sources of
VMS-Empire (version 1.13 and earlier), which are distributed under the
GNU General Public License, version 2. Large parts of this program are
direct translations of that C code, in particular:

  - the computer player (compmove.c and the path-finding in map.c),
    ported to src/ai.rs;
  - world generation, city placement and the choice of starting
    continents and difficulty levels (game.c), ported to src/game.rs;
  - the rules of movement, combat, city assault, production and
    retooling, fighter fuel, satellites and the endgame (object.c,
    attack.c, usermove.c, empire.c, data.c), reimplemented in
    src/game.rs and src/models.rs.

The terminal interface, save format, headless test rig and the
quality-of-life changes listed in README.md are new in this port. As
section 2(a) of the GPL asks, this file states that the program has been
modified; README.md ("Differences from the original") and CHANGELOG.md
describe the changes and when they were made.


LINEAGE AND CREDITS
-------------------

Empire was originally written by Walter Bright in the 1970s, in FORTRAN
on DEC PDP-10 systems.

The game was ported to DEC's VAX/VMS from the TOPS-10/20 FORTRAN sources
around fall 1979. Because it ran on VMS machines for so long, that
version became known as VMS Empire.

Ed James obtained the sources at Berkeley, converted parts of the code
to C to use curses for screen handling, and published his modified
sources in December 1986. Support for different terminal types was added
by Craig Leres; curses support by Ed James.

In early 1987 Chuck Simmons reverse engineered the program and wrote a
version entirely in C, with new algorithms, new commands and a redesigned
set of units. That C version is the ancestor of this port.
  Copyright (C) 1987, 1988 Chuck Simmons

Eric S. Raymond maintained the C version from around 1990 under the name
VMS-Empire: colorization, speed tuning, the save-interval option, ANSI C
cleanups, packaging, and the move from Chuck Simmons's modified GPL text
to the standard GNU GPL (version 1.8, 2010).

